% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We present a discrete-time simulator for a fleet of robots that serves
pickup and delivery tasks on a shelf grid, together with a planner and a
\emph{local plan-repair} engine that keeps the fleet running when the world
stops matching the plan.  The planner is layered: a partial-order planner
(POP) fixes \emph{what} each robot does and in which order; a space-time
A$^\ast$ search over a time-indexed reservation table fixes \emph{when} and
\emph{where} it moves; and a repair layer patches the plans of only the
robots whose goals or legs are actually invalidated when a lane closes, a
robot breaks down or one is called away to a spill.  Repair is a
modification rather than a re-plan: it never queries the global planner and
escalates through the rungs $R_0$--$R_4$, so a bad outcome can only be
\emph{local}.  Where a silent detour is impossible -- most clearly in the
single-door corridor that we use as a stress case -- the affected robots
\emph{negotiate} over a short message protocol
(\textsc{Propose}, \textsc{Accept}, \textsc{Reject}, \textsc{Commit})
that only grants a route through a booked cell if the holder agrees and the
grant pays for the robots it alters.  We report two case studies: a congested
$16\times16$ warehouse with 12 robots, six closed lanes, two breakdowns and
one diversion, and an $8\times10$ corridor whose single door makes a silent
detour impossible.  In the congested shift all five activity counters of the
visualisation are
non-zero: 12 of 12 robots altered at least once, 17 protocol messages,
9 disruptions, up to 3 closed cells and up to 3 queueing pairs at a time,
with all 24 tasks delivered.  A comparison across repair strategies shows
that the negotiated mode alters far fewer robots than the global re-planning
upper bound at a comparable makespan, and that the partial-order
representation is what keeps downstream robots merely \emph{delayed}
instead of re-planned.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents
\newpage



% =====================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% =====================================================================

A warehouse aisle is a shared resource.  Robots queue in it, pass each other
at its ends, and depend on each other's timing: a tote that one robot carries
is the precondition of another robot's next move.  Consequently the hard
problem is not finding a route for a single robot but keeping a \emph{fleet}
consistent while the world changes underneath it.  A closed cross-aisle, a
robot that stops working, or an urgent diversion to a spill all invalidate
part of the plan at a moment when the fleet is already moving, and a repair
that re-plans the whole fleet is neither necessary (most robots are
unaffected) nor affordable (the fleet would have to stop).

This report describes a planner and simulator for exactly that setting.  The
central design claim is that if the plan is stored as a \emph{partial order}
of robot actions rather than as a fixed timetable, then a disruption can be
repaired locally: the flaw is retracted, the retracted part is refined again
under a least-change objective, and the robots that are merely delayed by the
result are never touched.  When the local ladder cannot find a conflict-free
detour, a short negotiation between the robots that \emph{hold} the contested
cells produces one.

\subsection{Problem setting and assumptions}

A fleet of $N$ robots serves pickup/delivery tasks on a two-dimensional grid
with static shelf obstacles.  A task consists of a pickup cell and a delivery
cell; the fleet is given a set of tasks, and each robot executes its own plan
autonomously between repairs.

The model makes the following assumptions, all of which are enforced by the
simulator and the test suite:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Discrete space and time.}  The world is a unit-cell grid with
        4-neighbour moves.  One robot occupies one cell per tick; a tick is
        the unit of all scheduling decisions.
  \item \textbf{Reservations.}  A robot may use a cell or an edge only if it
        holds the corresponding time-indexed reservation (vertex, directed
        edge, and parked-robot reservation).
  \item \textbf{Bounded communication.}  A robot may only negotiate with
        robots inside a communication radius \code{comm\_radius}; the repair
        budget (ladder depth, number of passes, number of candidate robots)
        is bounded.
  \item \textbf{Locality of repair.}  A repair never calls the global planner
        and never re-plans a robot that is unaffected.  Only the disrupted
        robot, the robots that must wait for it, and the robots a grant
        actually alters may change their plan.
  \item \textbf{Frozen execution.}  Steps that have already started or
        finished are immutable; the repair works on the remaining suffix of
        the plan.
\end{itemize}

The rest of the report is organised as follows.  Section~\ref{sec:arch}
introduces the three-layer architecture and the module map.
Sections~\ref{sec:pocl}--\ref{sec:ground} describe the planner: the
partial-order layer, then the time-indexed grounding layer.
Section~\ref{sec:disruptions} models disruptions as plan flaws and
Section~\ref{sec:repair} presents the repair engine, the escalation ladder
and the negotiation protocol.  Section~\ref{sec:metrics} defines the
evaluation metrics, Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports the case studies
and the strategy comparison, and Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses the
results.  Section~\ref{sec:run} lists the commands that reproduce every
number in this report.

% =====================================================================
\section{System architecture}\label{sec:arch}
% =====================================================================

The implementation separates three concerns that are usually mixed in a
multi-robot planner: \emph{what} has to be done and in which order, \emph{when}
and \emph{where} it happens, and \emph{how the plan is changed} when the world
moves.  The separation is what makes a local repair possible, because the
repair layer can retract a single partial-order step and let the grounding
layer re-derive the affected legs only.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l l X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Module} & \textbf{Responsibility} \\
\midrule
L1: partial order & \code{pocl.py} &
  STRIPS operators, threat detection and resolution, best-first POP search;
  the plan is a partially ordered set of agent actions.
\\ \addlinespace
L2: grounding & \code{planner.py}, \code{peg.py}, \code{reservation.py} &
  space-time A$^\ast$ over the time-indexed reservation table, prioritized
  grounding, Plan-Execution-Graph retiming.
\\ \addlinespace
L3: repair & \code{modify.py}, \code{negotiation.py}, \code{metrics.py} &
  the five repair phases, the $R_0$--$R_4$ escalation ladder, the message
  protocol and the altered-agent metrics.
\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{The three layers of the planner and the modules that implement them.}
\label{tab:layers}
\end{table}

Around those three layers sit the modules that make the system runnable and
measurable: \code{world.py} (grid, shelves, task assignment and the
time-dependent blockages), \code{scenarios.py} (hand-built instances such as
the congested shift and the corridor), \code{disruptions.py} (event types,
injection and the postponement rule), \code{sim.py} (the tick loop and the
validator), \code{viz.py} and \code{viz\_plan.py} (grid animation, metrics
panel and the plan-space views: POP graph, Gantt chart, repair cards, ladder,
message-sequence diagram), \code{baselines.py} (the ablation strategies) and
\code{experiments.py} (the sweeps).

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l l X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Pipeline element} & \textbf{Warehouse meaning}\\
\midrule
action & \code{Goto} / \code{Pick} / \code{Drop} (L1) and \code{Move} / \code{Act} (L2, L3)\\
causal link & \code{at}, \code{holding}, \code{hand\_empty} (L1); a cell hand-over \code{Free(cell)} (L3)\\
ordering constraint & a precedence edge between two steps\\
threat & a new visit inside another robot's hand-over slot on the same cell\\
threat resolution & promotion/demotion, i.e.\ \emph{who waits for whom}\\
open condition & unsupported precondition (\code{operational(a)}, \code{delivered(o)})\\
exogenous step & \code{Block}/\code{Unblock} and \code{Break}/\code{Fix}\\
plan reuse & repair starts from the existing plan instead of a fresh search\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{Mapping between the classical partial-order planning vocabulary and the warehouse domain.}
\label{tab:mapping}
\end{table}

The data flow is a straight line, with the repair layer folded back into the
planner rather than placed after it:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=shell]
world.py -> pocl.py -> peg.py -> planner.py -> sim.py -> viz.py / viz_plan.py
             |                                 ^
             +-- repair: modify.py -------------+-- negotiation.py   (R0..R4)
                                |
                          disruptions.py / metrics.py
\end{lstlisting}

The planner is called \emph{once} per scenario to produce the initial plan;
after that the simulator only ever calls the repair engine, which is why a
scenario run is a genuine test of locality rather than of re-planning
quality.

% =====================================================================
\section{Layer 1: partial-order planning}\label{sec:pocl}
% =====================================================================

Each robot receives its own pop (partial-order plan).  Planning per robot is
sound here because robots do not share symbolic preconditions: the
interaction between robots is entirely spatial, and it is handled by the
grounding layer (Section~\ref{sec:ground}), not by the planner.

\subsection{STRIPS domain}

Facts are tuples, for example $(\text{at},a,\ell)$ (\emph{robot $a$ is at
cell $\ell$}), $(\text{holding},a,o)$ and $(\text{hand\_empty},a)$.  The
operators are the following.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l X X X c@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Action} & \textbf{Preconditions} & \textbf{Add} & \textbf{Delete} & \textbf{Duration}\\
\midrule
\code{Goto(a,x,y)} & \code{at(a,x)}, \code{operational(a)} & \code{at(a,y)} & \code{at(a,x)} & BFS dist.\\
\code{Pick(a,o,l)} & \code{at(a,l)}, \code{at\_item(o,l)}, \code{hand\_empty(a)} & \code{holding(a,o)} & \code{hand\_empty(a)}, \code{at\_item(o,l)} & 1\\
\code{Drop(a,o,l)} & \code{at(a,l)}, \code{holding(a,o)} & \code{delivered(o)}, \code{hand\_empty(a)} & \code{holding(a,o)} & 1\\
\code{Emergency(a,E)} & \code{at(a,E)}, \code{operational(a)} & \code{emergency\_done(a)} & --- & hold time\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{The STRIPS operator library.  Ground actions are instantiated only over the locations that matter for the robot (roughly $2\cdot\text{tasks}+2$ cells), which keeps the branching factor small.}
\label{tab:strips}
\end{table}

\subsection{Search}

The search is best-first over partial plans.  At every step a single
\emph{flaw} is chosen: threats first, then the open condition with the fewest
achievers.  An open condition is refined either by \emph{reuse} (an action
that is already in the plan achieves it) or by inserting a new action from
the library; a threat is resolved by \emph{promotion} or \emph{demotion},
i.e.\ by adding an ordering constraint.  The ranking function is
%
\[
  f(P) \;=\; g(P) + \bigl|\text{open}(P)\bigr|,
  \qquad
  g(P) \;=\; \sum_{\text{Goto}} \text{duration} \;+\; \#\{\text{non-Goto steps}\},
\]
%
so the search prefers plans that are cheap in travel and short in the number
of actions, with the number of remaining flaws as a tie-breaker towards
plans that are closer to being complete.

\subsection{Validity and soundness}

A candidate plan is accepted only if \code{is\_valid\_pop} holds:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item every precondition of every step is supported by a causal link,
  \item no threat remains between a link and an interfering step, and
  \item the ordering graph is acyclic.
\end{enumerate}

Validity is then \emph{executed} as a check: the plan is sampled twenty
times at random topological orders, each order is executed in the STRIPS
style from the initial state, and every sample must reach all goals.  This
catches the classic failure mode of acyclic-but-unsound plans (an ordering
that satisfies the graph but not the semantics), and it is cheap enough to
run inside the search loop.

The number of plans a repair has to consider is kept small by grounding only
over each robot's own reachable locations.  A plan that is valid for one
robot is therefore complete for that robot's own goals; the cross-robot
constraints are introduced afterwards as reservations.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{caseA_pop.png}
\caption{A partial-order plan of one of the robots that the repair altered in
case study~A.  Solid edges are ordering constraints, dashed edges are causal
links (\code{at}, \code{holding}, \code{hand\_empty}); the layered layout is
the topological depth.  Because the plan is a partial order, a delay only
moves the steps of the affected chain --- it does not force a new plan.}
\label{fig:pop}
\end{figure}

% =====================================================================
\section{Layer 2: time-indexed grounding}\label{sec:ground}
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Reservations and space-time A$^\ast$}

Grounding turns the partial order into a collision-free schedule.  The
shared resource is the \emph{reservation table}: a set of time-indexed
reservations over

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{vertices} --- robot $a$ occupies cell $c$ at tick $t$;
  \item \textbf{directed edges} --- robot $a$ traverses $c \to c'$ between
        $t$ and $t+1$ (the reverse edge is a different reservation, which is
        what makes swaps detectable); and
  \item \textbf{parks} --- a robot that is idle keeps its cell reserved
        until it moves.
\end{itemize}

A \emph{clash} is an attempt to reserve a vertex, edge or park that another
robot already holds.  Paths are found by space-time A$^\ast$ in the graph of
$(\text{cell}, \text{tick})$ states, with the Manhattan distance as the
admissible heuristic.  The search has two modes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{hard} --- clashed states are forbidden; used when a leg has
        fixed endpoints and must not disturb anybody (the self-reroute of
        $R_1$); and
  \item \textbf{soft} --- a clash is allowed but priced with
        \code{lambda\_soft}; used when the robot only needs to know
        \emph{which} cells and \emph{which} robots it would have to negotiate
        with.
\end{itemize}

The initial plan is produced by \emph{prioritized grounding}: robots are
ordered by priority (an emergency robot first, then the robot with the lower
id), and each robot is planned by hard space-time A$^\ast$ against the
reservations of the robots already committed.  Since every reservation is
time-indexed, mutual waiting is expressible: a robot that finds the door
occupied waits one tick and retries, which is exactly the behaviour a
congested aisle needs.

\subsection{The plan-execution graph (PEG)}

The grounded plan is stored as a plan-execution graph.  Nodes are
micro-steps; each has a never-reused identifier and a pointer to the
partial-order step it realises.  Edges carry the timing constraint:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{seq} --- consecutive micro-steps of the same robot, with weight
        equal to the duration of the source step;
  \item \code{logic} --- the \code{Pick} that makes a tote available to the
        matching \code{Drop}; and
  \item \code{res} --- a resource edge of weight \textbf{0}, added
        start-to-start between consecutive visitors of a cell.
\end{itemize}

For every cell $c$ the graph keeps the occupancy sequence
$\mathrm{occ}[c] = [(a_i, \text{enter}_i, \text{leave}_i)]$ sorted by
arrival, and for two consecutive visits of different robots it adds the
weight-0 edge $\text{leave}_p \to \text{enter}_q$.  An agent occupies a cell
from $\text{enter}+1$ until $\text{leave}$; the zero weight therefore lets a
robot \emph{follow} another into a cell on the very tick it is vacated,
which is the tight packing that a warehouse needs.

Weight-0 resource edges are why collisions can be detected on the graph
instead of on the schedule:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Collision theorem.}  A vertex swap, a head-on conflict and a
rotation conflict all appear as a cycle in the plan-execution graph.
Consequently \code{peg.is\_acyclic()} is a complete collision-safety check:
any assignment of times that respects every edge is free of vertex and swap
collisions.
\end{quote}

This turns an expensive pairwise schedule check into a single topological
test, which is what makes it affordable to run \emph{inside} every tentative
repair candidate, including the deep copies used during negotiation.

\subsection{Retiming}

\code{retime(peg, t\_now)} recomputes the schedule of a (possibly patched)
graph.  Steps that already started or finished keep their actual times, and
every other step is scheduled in topological order by
%
\[
  s.t \;=\; \max\Bigl(\{t_{\text{now}}\} \cup \{s.\text{anchor}\} \cup
  \{\,p.t + w(p,s) \;:\; p \in \text{preds}(s)\,\}\Bigr),
\]
%
where the anchor is only used when it is not \code{None}.  (The original
implementation used \code{s.anchor or t0}, which silently discards an anchor
of value $0$; the fix --- an explicit \code{is not None} test --- is one of
the invariants the test suite pins.)  Retiming is what makes a repair cheap:
a patched plan does not have to be re-grounded from scratch, it only has to
be re-timed.

\subsection{Validation}

The simulator validates every tick of every run, in every mode: a vertex
clash, a swap, a move into a shelf, a move into a blocked cell and a
collision with a parked robot are all violations, and the graph must
additionally be acyclic.  A repair that would produce a violation aborts and
rolls back to the previous plan.  The result of the validation is recorded
per run, and the comparison in Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports it
explicitly (the global upper bound, for instance, produces violations on the
congested instance even though it solves the task-set better in a sweep
setting).

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{caseA_gantt.png}
\caption{Per-robot Gantt chart of the repaired schedule of case study~A.
Rows are robots, columns are ticks; the colour is the robot's altered
category, so the yellow and orange rows are exactly the robots that the
ladder touched, while the grey rows only shifted in time.  The chart is the
visual counterpart of the altered-agent metrics of
Section~\ref{sec:metrics}.}
\label{fig:gantt}
\end{figure}

% =====================================================================
\section{Disruptions as plan flaws}\label{sec:disruptions}
% =====================================================================

A disruption is not modelled as an exception that interrupts a plan; it is
modelled as \emph{exogenous steps inserted into the plan}, which then leave
the plan in an invalid state.  The repair engine therefore never sees a
special case --- it sees a plan with flaws, exactly as the planner does.

All events of a scenario are derived from the \emph{undisrupted} plan
before any repair happens.  Every repair strategy (negotiated, self-only,
BFS-prioritised, global re-planning, and the no-repair ablation) therefore
sees the identical event list, and a comparison between strategies differs
in one variable only.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l l X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Event} & \textbf{Exogenous steps} & \textbf{Resulting flaws}\\
\midrule
Cell blockage & \code{Block(c)} at $t$, \code{Unblock(c)} at $t\!+\!D$ &
  later visits of $c$ must be ordered after \code{Unblock} (demotion $=$
  wait); if waiting costs more than the threshold, the leg is rerouted.\\
Breakdown & \code{Break(a)} at $t$, \code{Fix(a)} at $t\!+\!D$, \code{Fix}
  $\to$ next step of $a$ &
  \code{operational(a)} is violated for $D$ ticks; followers wait through
  \code{res} edges; a robot whose delay exceeds the threshold reroutes.\\
Permanent breakdown & \code{Break(a)} only &
  the remaining goals of $a$ become orphans (and \code{at\_item(o,cell)} if
  a tote is carried); visits to $a$'s cell must be rerouted.\\
Emergency & \code{Emergency(a,E)} before the remaining steps &
  an open condition \code{emergency\_done(a)} plus \code{at(a,E)}: the
  planner inserts the detour and restores the original \code{at} link.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{Every disruption class reduced to exogenous plan steps and the flaws those steps create.}
\label{tab:events}
\end{table}

The simulator replays the events in \textbf{tick order}, with three rules
that matter in practice:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item a repair pass only sees the events that were announced at or before
        its tick, so a later blockage can never be used to justify an
        earlier decision;
  \item a blockage is \emph{postponed} while it would trap a robot inside an
        already-executed step (a wall may not appear on a robot); and
  \item an accident hold is a \emph{constraint on the new plan}, never baked
        into the frozen prefix, so the already-executed part of the plan is
        not rewritten retrospectively.
\end{enumerate}

Residual safety violations are swept per time window, so a plan that is
consistent per tick cannot accumulate into an inconsistent schedule.

% =====================================================================
\section{Local plan repair}\label{sec:repair}
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Five phases}

A repair is a \emph{plan modification} in the style of
Kambhampati--Hendler: retract only the invalid parts, then refine with a
least-change search.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Diagnose.}  Copy the plan-execution graph, insert the
        exogenous steps, retime, and compute the affected set $A_0$ together
        with the flaw list: threatened resource links, unsupported
        preconditions, unreachable legs and orphan goals.
  \item \textbf{Unrefine.}  Retract \emph{only} what is invalid.  Threats
        that an ordering constraint can resolve retract nothing; a leg
        through a blocked cell retracts that leg's unstarted micro-steps; a
        permanent breakdown retracts the robot's unstarted steps.  Started
        and finished steps are never touched --- the executed prefix is
        re-rooted as the new start of the plan.
  \item \textbf{Refine and negotiate.}  Escalate through the ladder
        $R_0$--$R_4$ below, stopping at the first acceptable candidate.
  \item \textbf{Commit.}  Two-phase: assert acyclicity and run the simulator
        validator; otherwise abort and roll back to the previous plan.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The escalation ladder}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l X X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Rung} & \textbf{Operation} & \textbf{Who may change}\\
\midrule
$R_0$ & retime only (the new ordering edges \emph{are} the waiting) &
  robots that gain an incoming edge\\
$R_1$ & self-reroute of the affected \code{Goto} leg with \emph{hard}
  space-time A$^\ast$ (fixed endpoints), then insert the visit &
  the robot itself, plus the robots that now wait\\
$R_2$ & negotiation (Section~\ref{sec:negotiate}) within \code{comm\_radius},
  depth $\le$ \code{max\_depth} & the negotiation chain\\
$R_3$ & $R_2$ with \code{comm\_radius} $+\;3$ (at most twice) &
  the same, with a wider audience\\
$R_4$ & reassignment of orphan goals via POCL from the receiver's own plan &
  the broken robot and the receivers\\
fallback & keep $R_0$ (the robots wait), retry each tick; after
  \code{failure\_wait} ticks record \code{success = False} & ---\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{The escalation ladder.  The global planner is never called: the
worst case is \emph{everybody waits}, a correctness-preserving degradation
rather than a loss of locality.}
\label{tab:ladder}
\end{table}

Two acceptance rules govern the ladder.  First, a candidate is
\emph{acceptable} only if its total added delay does not exceed
\code{delay\_threshold}.  Second, and more importantly, candidates are
\emph{ranked} by a least-change cost
%
\[
  \text{cost}(c) \;=\; \text{added\_delay\_total}(c) \;+\;
  \beta_{\text{alter}} \cdot \bigl|\text{altered\_plan}(c)\bigr| .
\]
%
The weight $\beta_{\text{alter}}$ prices one altered robot in units of delay,
so a detour that is slightly longer but touches fewer robots wins.  The
lexicographic rule of the specification is the limit
$\beta_{\text{alter}} \to \infty$ of this cost.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{caseA_ladder.png}
\caption{The $R_0$--$R_4$ ladder with the rungs that the congested shift
actually used lit up.  In case study~A the ladder never has to widen the
communication radius and never needs a reassignment: waiting ($R_0$),
self-rerouting ($R_1$) and negotiation ($R_2$) are sufficient.}
\label{fig:ladder}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The negotiation protocol}\label{sec:negotiate}

Waiting is free and silent, but a detour that wants a cell another robot is
holding as a \emph{park} cannot be taken unilaterally.  That is what the
protocol is for: the repair asks the \emph{holder} of the contested cell for
permission, and the holder decides by whether the request pays for the robots
it alters.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=algo]
negotiate(i, depth, chain):
    path, C <- st_astar_soft(i, reservations)      # C = clashed holders
    if C is empty:
        apply path to a COPY of the PEG; return SUCCESS
    for j in C, in order of promise:
        if dist(i, j) > comm_radius or j in chain: continue
        PROPOSE_ORDER(i -> j, visit, link, before | after)
        j answers:
            1. grant is acyclic and delay(j) <= 2*delay_threshold -> ACCEPT(delay)
            2. else j reroutes its own leg, everybody else frozen -> ACCEPT(delay)
            3. else if depth < max_depth -> sub-negotiate, ACCEPT / REJECT
            4. else REJECT(reason)
    if every holder ACCEPTed:
        COMMIT; assert the patched PEG is acyclic -> SUCCESS
    else:
        ABORT; add every rejected clash as a HARD constraint; retry <= 3
\end{lstlisting}

Five properties of this protocol are worth stating explicitly, because they
are what keep the repair \emph{local}:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The proposal is a whole tour, not a cell.}  The initiator
        computes its remaining tour in \emph{soft} mode and the sink collects
        the set $C$ of holders it clashed with, so a negotiation is about a
        concrete route, and the answer \textsc{Reject} is informative rather
        than vague.
  \item \textbf{Acceptance by waiting is free.}  The robots that are replanned
        \emph{after} the initiator are re-derived against the initiator's new
        reservations, so they simply wait or reroute and need no further
        message.  This is why most negotiations alter very few robots.
  \item \textbf{A grant must pay.}  The initiator accepts a grant only when
        its own saving beats both the delay threshold and the
        $\beta_{\text{alter}}$ price of every robot that the grant newly
        alters.  A granted proposal is then verified clash-free against every
        non-yielding holder, so a grant can never introduce a collision.
  \item \textbf{Rejection is honest.}  A holder rejects if it is physically
        held (accident, breakdown or emergency hold), if its plan is frozen
        (outside the replan set, or already committed earlier in the same
        pass), or if the chain would close a cycle.  The rejected clash
        remains a hard constraint for the initiator, which then falls back to
        the hard space-time A$^\ast$ regrounding of the ladder.
  \item \textbf{Widening, not escalation to the global planner.}  A holder
        that is outside \code{comm\_radius} but inside
        $\code{comm\_radius} + 3\,(\code{max\_depth}-\text{depth})$ is pulled
        into the replan set, which is exactly rung $R_3$.
\end{itemize}

Every message (\textsc{Propose\_Order}, \textsc{Propose\_Reroute},
\textsc{Accept}, \textsc{Reject}, \textsc{Commit}, \textsc{Abort}) is
recorded, counted by type and exported into the repair record, the simulator
trace and the message arrows of the visualisation, which is why the panel
counter \emph{protocol messages} can be read directly from a run.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{caseA_dialogue.png}
\caption{Metrics panel (left) and protocol dialogue (right) for one pass of
case study~A.  The panel counts the five activity metrics of the
specification, and the transcript shows which robot asked whom for which
cell and how the holder answered.  A dialogue in which every arrow ends in
\textsc{Accept} means the route was granted by ordering (the holder waits),
i.e.\ nobody had to be re-planned.}
\label{fig:dialogue}
\end{figure}

% =====================================================================
\section{Measuring what a repair changed}\label{sec:metrics}
% =====================================================================

The headline question of the assignment --- \emph{how local is the repair?}
--- is only meaningful with a precise notion of ``changed''.  We therefore
report four nested readings of the same event, all computed on the
\emph{remaining} plan, i.e.\ on the steps strictly after the freeze tick
(so that editing the already-executed prefix can never flatter a repair).

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l l X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Signature compared} & \textbf{Meaning}\\
\midrule
\code{altered\_plan} \emph{(primary)} & action sequence and its content &
  the robot's \emph{plan} really changed: a new step, a lost step, a
  different action or a new ordering.\\
\code{altered\_path} & run-length-encoded cell route &
  the \emph{route} changed: a new cell is visited (or a task is reassigned).
  Inserting a wait never changes this signature.\\
\code{delayed\_only} & route identical, times different &
  the robot only waits: the cheapest and most desirable outcome of a
  repair.\\
\code{altered\_naive} & any differing $(\text{cell}, t)$ pair &
  the naive diff-the-trace definition; it counts a pure time shift as a
  change, and therefore upper-bounds the other three.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{The four altered-agent definitions.  They are ordered by
severity, and a repair that only produces \code{delayed\_only} agents has
not re-planned anybody.}
\label{tab:altered}
\end{table}

A repair pass also produces one \code{RepairRecord} per event:
(event type, tick, $|A_0|$, resolved rung, \code{altered\_plan\_ids},
\code{altered\_path\_ids}, \code{delayed\_ids}, \code{naive\_ids},
\code{added\_delay\_total}, \code{messages\_by\_type}, POP nodes, ST-A$^\ast$
calls, \code{cpu\_ms} and \code{success}).  The record is what the
visualisation prints on a repair card and what the sweeps aggregate.

\subsection{A worked example}

Consider two rows of the case study in Section~\ref{sec:caseA}.  At $t=29$ a
lane closes and the repair is resolved at rung $R_1$ (a pure self-reroute):
\code{altered\_plan} is $7$ while \code{altered\_path} and
\code{altered\_naive} are $8$.  The eighth robot is exactly the interesting
one --- its route is unchanged, only its timing moved, so the plan metric
excludes it while the naive trace diff would have counted it.  The single
additional tick of total delay that the pass reports ($\text{added\_delay}=1$)
is the same story at the fleet level: a lane closure that costs one tick is
a lane closure that almost nobody notices.

The largest pass is the $t=22$ accident: two robots stop on the same tick
while a lane closes, $A_0 = 8$, the repair resolves at $R_2$ after three
negotiated passes, and the pass ends with $17$ cumulative protocol messages
and $51$ ticks of added delay.  Reporting the mean over the pass
($\overline{\text{altered\_plan}} = 4$ of $12$ robots, $\max = 8$) alongside
the per-event table is what makes it visible that a \emph{single} accident
dominates the repair cost of the whole shift.

% =====================================================================
\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}
% =====================================================================

\subsection{Setup}

All results were produced on one machine with Python 3.12, NumPy, pandas and
Matplotlib, in a single deterministic configuration: every run is
seed-controlled, and the only non-reproducible column of any table is the
wall-clock \code{cpu\_ms} of a repair pass.  Three instance families are
used.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}l X c c X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Instance} & \textbf{Grid} & \textbf{Robots} & \textbf{Tasks} & \textbf{Disruptions}\\
\midrule
A: congested shift & $16\times16$, 49 shelves, one cross-aisle walled off
  (single door at $(8,8)$) & 12 & 24 & 6 closed lanes, 2 breakdowns,
  1 emergency ($=$ 9 events)\\
B: corridor choke point & $8\times10$, wall at column 3, single door at
  $(3,3)$ & 4 & 3 & 2 accidents (the parked holder and the robot it locks
  out)$^{a}$\\
Sweep: open warehouse & $30\times30$ & 5, 10 & 3 per robot &
  randomly placed closed lanes and breakdowns\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\caption{The three instance families.  $^{a}$Both halves of the corridor pair
are down early, so no hard candidate exists and the repair is forced onto the
negotiation rungs --- which is exactly what makes case~B the smallest
instance in which the protocol \emph{must} speak.}
\label{tab:instances}
\end{table}

The generator of a scenario is independent of the repair strategy: the event
list is derived once, from the undisrupted plan, and replayed for every
mode.  A comparison across modes therefore varies exactly one thing.

\subsection{Case study A: the congested shift}\label{sec:caseA}

One cross-aisle of the warehouse is closed for maintenance, so the fleet has
to funnel through a single door, and the shift opens with closed lanes, two
robots that stop at the same tick, and one robot that has to be diverted to a
spill.  This is the shift whose evolution
Figure~\ref{fig:finalpanel} shows at its final tick.

