tasks delivered   & 3 of 3\\
makespan          & 19 ticks\\
validated safe    & yes\\
repair passes     & 1 (rung $R_2$)\\
robots altered    & 2 (the parked holder and the locked-out robot)\\
protocol messages & 3 (\textsc{Propose\_Reroute}, \textsc{Accept}, \textsc{Commit})\\
FAILED\_INIT      & 0\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Case study B.  One pass, resolved at $R_2$, three messages, two
robots altered --- the minimum possible for a clash that no hard detour can
avoid.}
\label{tab:caseB}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}l c@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metrics-panel counter} & \textbf{Peak in this run}\\
\midrule
altered agents & 2\\
protocol messages & 3\\
disruptions & 2\\
blocked cells (peak) & 0\\
waiting pairs (peak) & 0\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The counters of the corridor run.  Note that ``blocked cells'' and
``waiting pairs'' are zero here: the corridor has no closed lane and no
queue, its disruption is purely the pair of accidents, so the counters and
the message log measure two different things and agree.}
\label{tab:countersB}
\end{table}

The dialogue of the pass is the textbook shape of the protocol: the initiator
proposes a reroute over the door, the holder answers that it can yield to it,
and the pass commits --- \textsc{Propose\_Reroute}, \textsc{Accept},
\textsc{Commit}, three messages, one altered robot pair.  No robot outside
the pair is touched, because no other robot's route crosses the door.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{caseB_sequence.png}
\caption{Message-sequence diagram of the corridor pass.  The vertical axis is
time, the horizontal axis is the pair of robots; each arrow is one protocol
message with its type and the cell it is about.  Reading the arrows is
reading the repair decision: the initiator asks first, the holder grants
second, and only the committing arrow changes a schedule.}
\label{fig:sequence}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{caseB_ladder.png}
  \caption{the ladder: only $R_2$ is used}\label{fig:caseB_ladder}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
  \centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{caseB_final_panel.png}
  \caption{the corridor at the final tick}\label{fig:caseB_final}
\end{subfigure}

\vspace{6pt}
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.85\textwidth}
  \centering\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{caseB_repair_card.png}
  \caption{the repair card of the pass ($t=2$, $R_2$, two altered robots)}
  \label{fig:caseB_card}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Case study~B in three views.  The ladder panel shows that $R_0$ and
$R_1$ were attempted and were not enough (the corridor offers no silent
detour), the final panel shows the two robots on either side of the door at
the end of the run, and the card records the message counts and the
before/after routes of the only pass.}
\label{fig:caseB}
\end{figure}

Comparing the two case studies isolates the role of the protocol.  Of the
eight passes of case~A, four end in a conversation (17 messages in total),
two are silent $R_1$ self-reroutes, and two are resolved by a longer detour
that turns out to need no conversation at all --- the ladder speaks only when
the geometry gives it no other choice.  In case~B the same ladder has no
choice at all: one clash, one conversation, three messages.  The protocol is
therefore not a general-purpose scheduler --- it is the escape hatch that
turns an impossible local detour into a two-robot agreement.

\subsection{Parameter sweep}

The two case studies are single instances.  To check that their message is
not an artefact of one carefully chosen warehouse, the same pipeline is run
over a grid of instance families: an open $30\times30$ warehouse with
$N \in \{5, 10\}$ robots, aisle-closure densities of $0\%$ and $5\%$, ten
seeds per cell, three tasks per robot and the same event generator, for
$40$ families per mode and $120$ runs in total.  Each run also emits one row
per disruption, so the sweep produces $1361$ disruption records on top of
the $120$ summary rows.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l c c c c c c c c@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{mode} & \textbf{plan} & \textbf{naive} & \textbf{cost} &
\textbf{makespan} & \textbf{succ.} & \textbf{safe} & \textbf{A$^\ast$} &
\textbf{msgs}\\
\midrule
global    & 5.047 & 5.498 & 1824.5 & 190.8 & 1.000 & 38/40 & 1251.4 & 0.00\\
negotiate & 1.572 & 1.634 & 2083.8 & 221.0 & 0.920 & 40/40 &  100.5 & 0.00\\
self\_only & 1.150 & 1.150 & 2085.7 & 220.9 & 0.931 & 40/40 &   45.9 & 0.00\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-mode means over the $40$ families of the sweep (three tasks per
robot, $30\times30$).  Columns: the mean \code{altered\_plan} and
\code{altered\_naive} count per event, the sum of costs, the makespan,
\emph{succ.} $=$ the fraction of disruptions repaired within the budget,
\emph{safe} $=$ the families in which \emph{no} tick of the run produced a
validation violation, \emph{A$^\ast$} $=$ the mean number of space-time
searches and \emph{msgs} the mean number of protocol messages.}
\label{tab:sweep}
\end{table}

The sweep says four things.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The locality advantage is real, and it is about a factor of
        three.}  A negotiated repair alters $1.57$ robots per event on
        average against $5.05$ for the global restart; the median instance
        alters $1.50$, $22$ of the $40$ families stay at or below $1.5$,
        $35$ of $40$ stay at or below $2$, and $38$ of the $40$ families are
        at least as local as the global mode.  The mean ratio of the global
        altered-set size to the local one is $3.29$.
  \item \textbf{Locality is paid for in travel, not in correctness.}  The
        sum of costs rises from $1825$ to $2084$ ($+14\%$) and the makespan
        from $191$ to $221$, which is the expected price of not being
        allowed to re-plan the fleet; but every negotiated and self-only run
        is conflict-free, while the global upper bound is not
        ($38/40$ families, two runs with residual conflicts).  The number of
        space-time searches does the same: $100$ for local repair against
        $1251$ for the restart, i.e.\ the locality also buys back most of the
        planner's work.
  \item \textbf{Failures degrade into waiting, never into collisions.}  In
        $8$ of the $40$ negotiated families one robot does not finish its
        task with the default retry budget (a mean success rate of $0.92$,
        the worst family at $0.50$).  Every one of those runs is still
        conflict-free: an exhausted ladder keeps the robot waiting
        ($R_0$ fallback) and records \code{success = False}, which is
        precisely the failure mode the design wants --- slower, never unsafe.
  \item \textbf{The protocol stays silent in an open warehouse.}  The mean
        number of messages is $0.00$ in all three modes.  On a $30\times30$
        grid with sparse blockages the hard detour of $R_1$/long detour of
        $R_2$ is always available, so no robot ever has to ask another for
        permission.  This is the reason both case studies use a constrained
        instance: the protocol is only observable where the geometry makes a
        silent detour impossible.
\end{itemize}

