\caption{The sweep in six views.  The gap between the global restart and the
local modes in (a)--(b) is the locality result; (c)--(d) show that the price
is a mild increase in travel that grows with $N$; (e) shows which rung
resolved each pass --- the two local modes stay on the low rungs, while every
pass of the global baseline is a full restart, which is why its bar is the
largest; and (f) shows the four altered-agent definitions separating as the
theory predicts --- the naive trace diff is the largest and the plan metric
the smallest.}
\label{fig:sweep}
\end{figure}

% =====================================================================
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
% =====================================================================

\subsection{What the partial order buys}

The measurements support the design claim in a specific, checkable form: the
difference between the plan metric and the naive trace metric is exactly the
set of robots whose \emph{timing} changed but whose \emph{plan} did not, and
that set is non-empty precisely because the plan is a partial order.  A
timetable representation has no such set: every time a robot waits, its
``plan'' changes, and a re-scheduling is indistinguishable from a re-planning.
The $t=29$ row of Table~\ref{tab:passesA} (plan $7$ vs path $8$ for an added
delay of one tick) is the smallest example of the phenomenon, and
Figure~\ref{fig:sw_defs} shows the same ordering across the whole sweep.

\subsection{Honesty about failure}

Three failure modes are reported rather than hidden.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{An exhausted ladder.}  In the sweep, $8$ of $40$ negotiated
        families end with one robot unfinished.  The run is still
        conflict-free: the robot waits, the record says
        \code{success = False}, and the fleet keeps delivering.  A local
        repair can be \emph{incomplete} --- it cannot promise to finish every
        task under every budget --- but it can promise never to become
        unsafe, and that is the trade the design makes.
  \item \textbf{A disconnected map.}  If a disruption permanently removes the
        last edge between two halves of the warehouse, no local repair can
        undo it: the affected task is simply unreachable, and the only honest
        answer is to hand the goal to a robot on the other side (rung $R_4$)
        or to fail loudly.  Both of those are implemented, and the
        hand-off path is exercised by the BFS baseline in
        Table~\ref{tab:compare}.
  \item \textbf{A global restart is not a safe upper bound.}  The most
        surprising result of the comparison is that the strategy with the
        most freedom produces the only violations and the only incomplete
        task set in the congested instance.  This is a property of the
        \emph{executed prefix}, not of the search: once a plan is running, a
        fleet-wide restart is not available in any meaningful sense, and
        pretending otherwise produces reservations that the past has already
        spent.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}

The evaluation has three limitations worth stating.  First, the sweeps use a
fixed grid of $N \in \{5, 10\}$ at two densities: the trend is clear, but the
$N$ range is small and the largest instance ($N=10$ on $30\times30$) is far
from a real warehouse fleet.  Second, \code{cpu\_ms} is wall-clock and
therefore not reproducible; every other column of every table is.  Third, the
negotiation is evaluated with one fixed budget
(\code{max\_depth}, \code{delay\_threshold}, \code{beta\_alter}); the
sensitivity of the number of messages and of the altered sets to those knobs
is a natural next experiment (the code exposes standalone sweeps for the
communication radius and for the POP node budget, which is where such a study
would start).

% =====================================================================
\section{Reproducing the results}\label{sec:run}
% =====================================================================

\textbf{Environment.}  Python 3.10 or newer (the results here were produced
with 3.12), with NumPy, pandas and Matplotlib; \code{pytest} for the test
suite and \code{ffmpeg} optionally for MP4 export.  Nothing in the pipeline
needs a GPU and no step needs network access.  The sources, the test suite and
every artefact referenced in this report live at
\url{https://github.com/aaditya-biswas/AS_Assignment}.

\textbf{Determinism.}  There is a single seeded random stream per run, every
set or dictionary iteration that feeds a decision is sorted, and the whole
suite is meant to be run with \code{PYTHONHASHSEED=0}.  Every table in this
report is reproduced exactly by the commands below; the only column that is
not reproducible is the wall-clock \code{cpu\_ms} of a repair pass.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=shell]
# 1. the congested shift of case study A (12 robots, 16x16, 9 events)
python run_demo.py

# 2. the same shift, every repair strategy on the identical event list
python run_demo.py --compare

# 3. the same shift with the figures of this report
python run_demo.py --compare --viz --outdir outputs

# 4. the corridor of case study B (the protocol is forced to speak)
python run_demo.py --choke --compare --viz --outdir outputs/choke

# 5. the parameter sweep (results.csv + the plots of the sweep figure)
python experiments.py --sweep grid --seeds 10 --plots

# 6. the test suite
PYTHONHASHSEED=0 python -m pytest -q
\end{lstlisting}

The test suite contains 110+ tests; the ones that matter for the claims of
this report are the multi-seed safety tests (\code{test\_peg\_sim.py},
\code{test\_planner.py}), which assert over many seeds that a completed run
has \emph{no} validation violations, that every task is delivered and that
every robot ends parked on its own parking cell --- the invariant that a
repair may never violate.



