% =====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% =====================================================================

We built a planner and simulator for a multi-robot warehouse and used them to
test one claim: that a disruption can be repaired \emph{locally} when the plan
is kept as a partial order, and that where a local detour is geometrically
impossible, a short negotiation between the robots that hold the contested
cells can supply the missing freedom.  The evidence is consistent across two
carefully chosen instances and a $120$-run sweep: a negotiated repair alters
about a third as many robots per event as a fleet-wide restart, spends a
fraction of the search effort, never produces a validation violation in the
sweep, and on the congested instance is the only strategy that finishes the
task set.  The global restart --- the strategy that is \emph{unconstrained}
by locality --- ends with violations and three undelivered tasks, because it
cannot respect the part of the plan that has already executed.  The two case
studies then show the protocol at work: silent when the geometry allows a
detour, and three messages when it does not.

The natural next steps are the ones the limitations suggest: push $N$ and the
load into a regime where the ladder's failure mode is common rather than
rare, sweep the negotiation budget and the communication radius as
stand-alone axes, and let the repair negotiate about \emph{goals} as well as
about cells --- a task hand-off agreed between two robots is the obvious
generalisation of the $R_4$ rung.


